CHANGE ANALYSIS (citations verified, indeterminate)
───────────────────────────────────────────────────
Cannot be established from this repository alone: depends on state only known at runtime (e.g. feature flags, request data).
The mechanism is explicit, but its observable effect depends on runtime context initialization paths, the database response to CURRENT_CATALOG(), existing bootstrap settings, and whether the returned catalog can be resolved in loaded metadata.

Databricks connections now use a dedicated execution context that discovers and tracks the server's current catalog, can seed an empty bootstrap catalog setting, and copies a default catalog into derived contexts. Catalog-selection failures are logged and suppressed rather than propagated.

Likely behavioral changes
  • New Databricks execution contexts use the Databricks-specific implementation; an independently initialized context refreshes defaults, including querying the database for its current catalog.
  • When the current-catalog query returns a nonempty name, the context tracks that catalog and, when bootstrap settings are in use, fills an otherwise empty bootstrap default-catalog name. Default-catalog lookup then resolves the tracked name through the data source.
  • A context initialized from another Databricks context attempts to select the source context's default catalog rather than independently refreshing the server defaults.
  • If the inherited catalog-selection operation throws DBCException, callers of the Databricks override no longer receive that failure; it is recorded only as a debug log, and the tracked catalog name is updated only after a successful superclass call.

Investigate
  • Trace execution-context construction and initialization, including cloned, metadata, and auxiliary contexts, to verify the Databricks-specific type and source-context assumptions.
  • Trace consumers of default-catalog state and bootstrap settings to determine whether the discovered catalog affects navigation, SQL qualification, reconnect behavior, or persisted connection settings.
  • Inspect catalog-switch callers and framework contracts to determine whether suppressing DBCException can leave callers believing a catalog change succeeded.

Uncertain
  • The supplied context does not show which lifecycle paths invoke createExecutionContext and initializeContextState, or which kinds of Databricks sessions receive the new behavior.
  • It is not established that every context and initFrom value reaching initializeContextState was created as DatabricksExecutionContext; inherited or alternate construction paths must be checked against the casts.
  • The diff does not establish how supported Databricks JDBC/server versions handle SELECT CURRENT_CATALOG(), nor whether failures or result shapes vary by driver or server version.
  • The ownership, persistence, and sharing semantics of the DBPConnectionBootstrap object returned by getBootstrapSettings are not shown, so it is unclear whether setting its default catalog is context-local, connection-wide, or persisted.
  • The actual server current-catalog value, whether it is nonempty, and whether the corresponding catalog is present in loaded metadata cannot be established from the diff.

